MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This question sort of pertains to R/QTLBIM mostly but I think is
general enough to ask it here.  I'd like to compare my Arabidopsis RIL
analyses using scantwo in R/QTL to the 2 way genome scans using R/
QTLBIM.  The problem is that qtlbim doesn't recognize the class
'riself', so I'll have to analyze my recombinant inbred line data as
if it were a back cross population.  What if any problems will I have
interpreting the qtlbim output?  Specifically, will the qtl number,
position, effect strength, or significance tests for additive and
additive x additive QTL be incorrect when analyzing RIL data as a back-
cross?   If not, why do software packages like R/QTL and QTL
cartographer have separate designations from RIL and BC cross types
when entering your data?

Thanks!

Tarek Elnaccash

